WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:05.077
When a scalar query feels confusing, isolate the inner query mentally.

00:00:05.077 --> 00:00:09.682
Ask what it returns. Here it returns one average age.

00:00:09.682 --> 00:00:13.432
Now imagine replacing the parenthesized SELECT with that number.

00:00:13.432 --> 00:00:17.302
The outer query becomes an ordinary age comparison.

00:00:17.302 --> 00:00:22.052
This simple mental substitution works well for many scalar subqueries.

00:00:22.052 --> 00:00:25.497
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:25.497 --> 00:00:33.032
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:35.012
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:35.012 --> 00:00:43.347
In a real setting such as a hospital identifying patients above a benchmark or with no follow-up,

00:00:43.347 --> 00:00:48.512
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:48.512 --> 00:00:51.382
before discussing syntax.

00:00:51.382 --> 00:00:57.811
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
